{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nLLM failure classification is duplicated in exactly ONE place: the context-length phrase list is\nbyte-identical in `src/rebar/llm/failure.py:148-157` and\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sizing.py:316-329`; `failure.py:144-147` records why (\"kept local so this\nlow-level boundary module has no dependency on the plan_review package — the two must stay in\nsync\"). A fix to one silently leaves the other stale — the instalment pattern the review found.\n\n## Approach\n**Scope reduction — read this first.** The genuine content is ONE duplication: the eight-phrase\n`_CONTEXT_LEN_HINTS` tuple copied byte-for-byte into `sizing.py`. The other three consolidations the\noriginal plan proposed are WITHDRAWN, not deferred: the retryable→exit-code mapping is ALREADY\ncentralised in `_cli/_llm_commands.py:45` (`_llm_error_exit_code`) and `:64-100`\n(`_disposition_exit_code`), called by three verbs (`:147`, `:157`, `:231`, `:336`) plus\n`_commands/claim.py:120-123` and `_commands/composer.py:778-781`, so there is no fourth hand-rolled\ncopy; the `structured.py` message merge is harmful and was decided against in a closed, merged bug\n(see Deliberate divergences); and the MCP payload helper composes no exit code at all.\nWhat remains is a one-line import change plus its guard.\n\n`sizing.is_context_limit_error` stops carrying its own tuple and reads `failure._CONTEXT_LEN_HINTS`\ninstead. The `def is_context_limit_error` MUST STAY in `sizing.py` — `orchestrator.py:72` binds\n`_is_context_limit_error = sizing.is_context_limit_error` at import time and\n`test_plan_review_prerequisites_model_log.py:45` monkeypatches the name as a string, so a re-export\nin its place would desynchronise both. The direction `sizing → failure` introduces no cycle\n(`failure.py` imports only `rebar.llm.errors`; `sizing.py:26-32` already imports `rebar.llm.config`,\n`.errors`, `.model_classes`, `.runner`) and preserves the boundary constraint `failure.py:144`\nrecords.\n\nGuard: the eight-phrase CONJUNCTION appears in exactly one file. Match the co-occurrence of all\neight literals within a single expression (per the epic's rule 2 — never a single common token such\nas `\"context\"`), exempt the owner `src/rebar/llm/failure.py`, honour a `# context-hints-ok: <reason>`\nline marker.\n\n### Deliberate divergences this story must NOT collapse\n- `workflow/completion_failures.py:46-79` `_is_token_exhaustion` is a THIRD, intentionally different\n  classifier: it omits the bare `\"context\"` substring the other two carry (\"without mistaking\n  generic context text\") and keys off a normalized `finish_reason` set (`:52-60`). Folding it in\n  would make any message containing the word \"context\" read as token exhaustion.\n- `structured._BAD_STOP_REASONS` (`structured.py:616-627`) is NOT a copy of `failure.message_for`\n  (`failure.py:355`): it is keyed by finish/stop reason (`refusal`/`max_tokens`/`length`/\n  `content_filter`/`error`) with EXCEPTION text as values (raised at `structured.py:646,648,676`),\n  while `message_for` is keyed by the disjoint 8-member `ResolutionClass` vocabulary and returns\n  operator remedy prose. The key spaces share no member, so `message_for(\"refusal\")` is `None` and\n  `check_stop_reason` would raise with no message. Bug `injurious-definable-badger` (closed, landed\n  `0e41876787` via Gerrit 1525) decided this: \"Leave `check_response`'s raise text unchanged.\"\n- `_llm_commands.py:188-190` (`scan-spec`) returns a flat exit **1** on `LLMError`; its MCP twin\n  `_mcp_llm.py:109-133` has no `except` at all. Neither joins the disposition mapping.\n- `_llm_commands.py:244-246` (`verify-completion`) carries an extra rule from bug 2a6f\n  (`verdict_obtainable is False → 11`) and no INDETERMINATE→2 arm.\n- `close_precheck.py:691-714` additionally calls `failure.log_degrade` and\n  `failure.close_gate_remedy`, and embeds the message in a `CommandError` instead of writing\n  `llm-degrade: …` to stderr as the CLI helpers do.\n- `_disposition_exit_code` reads `result[\"verdict\"]` (both the plan-review string and the nested\n  code-review dict) and calls `plan_review.resign.classify_plan_review_attestation`; neither is\n  reachable from an `LLMOutcome` or a `coverage` dict, so no `exit_code_for(...)` can replace it.\n\n## Guard construction\nFollow the guard-test construction rule in the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner module exempt, match the constituent conjunction or AST shape named in Approach (or the definition count where no distinctive atom exists), `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape marker, and behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sizing.py` (drop the local tuple, import the owner's);\n`src/rebar/llm/failure.py` (comment update only — the tuple is already correct);\n`tests/unit/test_context_hints_single_source.py` (new; flat, as `tests/unit/llm/` does not exist).\n\nExplicitly OUT of scope, for the reasons above: `src/rebar/llm/structured.py`,\n`src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`, `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py`,\n`src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_failures.py`.\n\n## Testing\nGuard: the conjunction resolves to one file under `src/rebar` with the owner exempt, and is proven\nto FLAG by reintroducing a second copy in a temp fixture, not just to pass. Namesakes it must\nleave untouched: `run_failure.py:190-191`, `enrich.py:47` (docstrings quoting the phrases as prose),\n`completion_failures.py:56,59,73`.\n\nPinning — must pass with no assertion edits, all under `tests/unit/`:\n`test_llm_failure_classifier.py` (`:188-211`, both classifiers agree on a wrapped context error,\ndisagree on a refusal);\n`test_change_input_not_an_outage_43d4.py::test_the_new_prefix_does_not_itself_look_like_a_context_limit`\n(`:245-266`, the sharpest guard on this tuple);\n`test_plan_review.py::test_is_context_limit_error_and_ladder` (`:1331`, reaches the predicate via\nthe import-time alias `orchestrator._is_context_limit_error`);\n`test_plan_review_prerequisites_model_log.py` (`:45`, monkeypatches `sizing.is_context_limit_error`\nby string — the `def` must survive); `test_llm_failure_matrix.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The eight-phrase list has exactly one definition under `src/rebar`; checked by\n  `grep -rln 'too many tokens' src/rebar` returning exactly `src/rebar/llm/failure.py`, and by the\n  new guard test.\n- [ ] `sizing.is_context_limit_error` still exists as a `def` in `plan_review/sizing.py` and reads\n  the owner's tuple; checked by\n  `grep -c 'def is_context_limit_error' src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sizing.py` returning `1` and\n  `grep -n '_CONTEXT_LEN_HINTS' src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sizing.py` returning a hit.\n- [ ] The three legitimate namesakes survive; checked by `grep -rln 'token limit' src/rebar`\n  returning exactly `llm/failure.py` and `llm/run_failure.py`, and by\n  `grep -c 'context_length_exceeded' src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_failures.py` returning `3`.\n- [ ] `completion_failures.py::_is_token_exhaustion` is unchanged; checked by\n  `git diff origin/main -- src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_failures.py` being empty.\n- [ ] No exit-code or message behaviour changes; checked by `git diff origin/main` over\n  `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py`,\n  `src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py` and `src/rebar/llm/structured.py` being empty.\n- [ ] The guard FAILS when a second copy is introduced, not merely passes clean; checked by the\n  guard test's own negative case.\n- [ ] The five pinning suites named in Testing pass with no assertion edits; checked by `pytest`\n  over them and by `git diff --stat origin/main -- tests/` showing only additions.\n- [ ] Net non-test line count is negative (the epic's consolidation guarantee); checked by\n  `git show --numstat` on this story's commit, excluding tests."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787371181391145001,"uuid":"21005cb7-8c06-4169-b2b9-f42e70b43596"}